3892822265625, -0.0262451171875, 0.00872802734375, -0.00925445556640625, -0.019012451171875, -0.01050567626953125, 0.00494384765625, -0.014129638671875, -0.0028705596923828125, 0.0213165283203125, -0.006168365478515625, 0.0009179115295410156, -0.0090789794921875, 0.0235137939453125, 0.0148468017578125, 0.057098388671875, 0.0035247802734375, 0.01861572265625, -0.03741455078125, -0.02459716796875, 0.0223541259765625, -0.006679534912109375, 0.017486572265625, 0.00955963134765625, -0.0323486328125, -0.032989501953125, 0.01561737060546875, 0.00037550926208496094, 0.00992584228515625, 0.0230560302734375, -0.01141357421875, -0.0031032562255859375, 0.0120391845703125, 0.01129913330078125, 0.005298614501953125, 0.0155487060546875, 0.0125885009765625, -0.006378173828125, -0.034576416015625, -0.0092010498046875, -0.0197296142578125, 0.01007843017578125, 0.007587432861328125, -0.01097869873046875, 0.032257080078125, -0.024078369140625, -0.022216796875, 0.01056671142578125, 0.00213623046875, 0.0249176025390625, -0.04107666015625, -0.004352569580078125, 0.00450897216796875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Delegating cognitive operations to generative artificial intelligence redistributes task execution and presents a governance challenge: determining the appropriate degree of ongoing human involvement. We distinguish between two approaches. Specified delegation involves human input prior to execution, through instructions, constraints, or criteria that define the task. In contrast, iterative coproduction entails human intervention during execution, enabling correction or redirection of provisional outputs as they are generated.\n\nTo analyze these approaches, we use aggregate monthly data from the Anthropic Economic Index (AEI) for April and May 2026. The AEI differentiates between two usage modes: 1P API, representing direct traffic through Anthropic's API, and Claude.ai, which aggregates activity from Chat and Cowork. Using this data, we test whether a stronger work-related orientation in human-AI interaction is associated with increased specified delegation within each mode, and whether the rise in the iterative profile is greater in Claude.ai than in 1P API.\n\nThe main analysis uses level-0 O*NET tasks and estimates how both profiles shift when an eligible record reallocates ten percentage points from personal to work-related use. The iterative comparison is limited to 1,411 node-month pairs observed and eligible in both modes. Specified delegation increases by 2.76 points in 1P API (95% CI: [2.30, 3.22]) and by 1.45 in Claude.ai (95% CI: [0.93, 1.97]). On the common support, iterative coproduction changes by -0.30 points in 1P API and by 0.15 in Claude.ai, resulting in a between-mode difference of 0.45 points (95% CI: [0.15, 0.75]).\n\nThese results indicate that a work-related orientation is associated with stronger evidence of prior human direction, and that the observable iterative response varies across usage modes. The article shifts the focus from the extent of AI execution to the timing of observable human direction."}}